<!--
  ZITLAS notification small icon.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Android renders a notification's SMALL icon as a
  silhouette from API 21 onward: every pixel is discarded except its alpha
  channel, then re-filled with the system tint. `@mipmap/ic_launcher` — which
  is what this app was passing, and what FCM falls back to when the manifest
  names no icon — is a full-colour, fully-opaque square, so its alpha channel
  is a solid block. The result on the lock screen and in the shade is a plain
  white square: the "generic Android blob" that makes an app look unfinished.

  So a notification icon has to be authored as alpha only. This is that icon:
  the bolt-Z from assets/icon/logo_icon.png, drawn white on transparent.

  WHAT WAS DROPPED, AND WHY. The full logo also has three concentric orbit
  arcs. They are ~1px wide once scaled to 24dp and vanish into aliasing at
  status-bar size, and stroked hairlines are exactly what the silhouette pass
  destroys. The bolt-Z is the element that survives monochrome flattening and
  stays recognisable at 24dp, so it carries the identity alone here. The full
  colour mark still appears as the LARGE icon / app icon, where it has the
  pixels to work.

  Geometry is a closed polygon, not a stroke, for the same reason: fills
  survive the alpha pass at any density; thin strokes do not.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!--
      The bolt-Z: top bar, a lightning diagonal that kinks left of centre,
      then the bottom bar. Traced anticlockwise from the top-right corner.
      Bars are 4.2 units deep so they hold their weight when the system
      downscales this to 18dp inside the status bar.
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M18.9,3.1
                          L6.1,3.1
                          L6.1,7.3
                          L12.6,7.3
                          L9.9,11.4
                          L14.3,11.4
                          L4.7,20.9
                          L17.5,20.9
                          L17.5,16.7
                          L11.0,16.7
                          L13.7,12.6
                          L9.3,12.6
                          Z" />
</vector>
